```nginx
location ~ ^/post/([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)$ {
  try_files $uri.html =404;
}
```
This location block uses a regular expression to match URLs that start with `/post/` followed by a slug (i.e., a string of characters, underscores, or hyphens). If a matching file exists, Nginx will serve it; otherwise, it will return a 404 error.

3. Create a directory in your `usr/share/nginx/html` directory to store your post files. For example, create a directory named `posts`.

4. When a new post is created or existing posts are updated, make sure to update the corresponding HTML files in the `posts` directory. These files should follow the same structure as the `post-template.html` file:
```html
<a href="/post/<%= slug %>">
  <%= title %>
</a>
```
For example, if a post has a slug `my-first-post` and title `Hello World`, the corresponding HTML file would be `posts/my-first-post.html` with the following content:
```html
